aven't been discussed. Perhaps we can argue that Candidate D has strong responsibility and problem solving, but concerns about being a know-it-all and hot temper. Candidate A has strong technical and leadership but is a show-off and not open to new ideas. We need to weigh.\n\nGiven the criteria, reliability is crucial. We have no explicit reliability for A or D. B is very conscientious (reliability). C is 100% reliability (implied). D no reliability fact. A no reliability fact. So reliability favors B and C. Stress resilience: B handles stress well, C keeps calm in crisis, D responds adequately to unexpected events, A unknown. Decision quality: A anticipates dangerous situations, sees complex connections, C makes correct decisions quickly, D solves problems extremely well. Technical competence: A excellent spatial vision, sees complex connections, D concentrates, solves problems, B good at assessing weather, computer skills, C understands complicated technology. Crew cooperation: C creates positive crew atmosphere, B pretentious, wrong tone, D know-it-all, hot temper, A show-off, not open to new ideas. Communication: B pretentious, wrong tone, C difficulty communicating ideas, D hot temper, A show-off, not open to new ideas. Responsibility: D takes responsibility seriously, A leadership qualities, B conscientious, C puts concern for others above everything (maybe responsibility). Adaptability: C not willing to further education, B unknown, A not open to new ideas, D hot temper, know-it-all.\n\nOverall, C has reliability, crew atmosphere, quick decisions, but communication and adaptability concerns. B has reliability, stress handling, technical, but communication issues (pretentious). D has responsibility, problem solving, but communication and crew cooperation issues. A has technical and leadership but poor openness and show-off.\n\nThus likely best is between B and C. Need to decide. Perhaps we can argue that C's egocentric and unwillingness to further education is